  "id": "Compound::DB01041 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Thalidomide is implicated in the development of hematologic cancer through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Thalidomide may contribute to the onset of hematologic cancer by inducing demyelination, which is mediated through Nelarabine, an agent that in turn targets hematologic cancer. Similarly, Thalidomide is linked to the development of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), which is associated with Dasatinib or Imatinib, both of which are used to treat hematologic cancer. These findings highlight a potential role of Thalidomide in the pathogenesis of hematologic malignancies, possibly through its effects on cellular processes that lead to cancer development. The integration of these pathways underscores the complex interplay between Thalidomide and hematologic cancer, emphasizing the importance of considering multiple mechanisms in the context of drug reutilization. The evidence, while not definitive, suggests that Thalidomide may have a role in the progression of hematologic cancer, particularly in the context of certain malignancies that are treated with agents like Nelarabine, Dasatinib, and Imatinib.",
